1. Math Notes feel not quite right
   - Doubleclick to 'click in' feels inconsistent with vim idiom
      - It'd be cool if, when selected but not like 'entered':
         - 'i' would enter and open insert mode
         - ':' would enter and start an ex command
   - No resize handles, or perhaps handles so strangely placed they're usually offscreen
      - This is the big issue.
      - Maybe it'd be cool if these resized automatically to fit text
         - They'd start short---like height=width/3---and grow as you type
         - We'd then have a like 'lock size' button on the note's bottom bar
   - Invisible until you place them
      - afaik stickies sort of have a shadow you move around until they're dropped. That'd be cool
   - Not integrated with tldraw toolbar color chooser
      - although the current bottom-of-note-bar thing is actually pretty good
   - When away from my home network using my ipad, notes wouldn't stay moved
      - i'd drag them and they'd snap back to their original location. Race condition?
      - clicking into them using finger/ipad trackpad didn't seem to work consistently either, although pen did
   - Content seems to be truncated at least when accessed some ways
      - I've had agents actually take a screenshot of the note and then OCR it to read the content, complaining that the content was truncated.

3. There should be some like highlight for newly changed content. Think like, unread emails are highlighted in your inbox. Often agents will make changes places i'm not expecting them to and i'll be convinced there is an issue with the watcher/rebuild server because i'm not seeing the changes i'm expecting. A subtle in-text highlight and 'recent changes' list in the TOC would be helpful. Perhaps clicking on the highlight either in text or in the TOC would remove it from the list, and the list could have a like select all/mark read kind of feature.

4. Things seem a little brittle. New features break old ones, etc.
   - There should be an audit to explore whether maybe different approaches could be unified, e.g. places where manual coordinate calculations could be replaced with TLDraw-native calculations.
   - Similarly, there should be an audit to explore whether there are multiple code paths doing essentially the same thing.
   - Perhaps we should think about whether sync info, etc should be stored in some kind of light DB vs. flat files for atomicity, faster search, etc.

5. SVG/text highlight layer / ref clicking calibration seems a little imperfect. Perhaps there should be a calibration procedure performed page by page by the watcher to really nail down those offsets.
6. The ref clicking for the def table via sync table is a bit janky. Is it not possible to like, use hyperref links in the TeX in the SVG itself to do some of this, for a more native LateX feel?
7. The bib handling seems a little rough. Sometimes the watcher doesn't run bibtex even when there are undefined refs that are available in bibtex. This should be fixed.
8. No in-viewer error reporting. If something needs correcting by the user, it'd be good to get some indication in the ui. Perhaps the watcher can throw us a sticky note where we're looking, or --- if we want to be really persistent --- have one track the user. Actually, that'd be a cool feature---like having stickies be able to like, transiently float in a fixed screen position, then settle back into doc position.
9. TeXsync from browser->editor/pdf. It'd be cool if we could do this via our texsync url or some other means.

10. Favicon. Let's try a bold white semicolon (tldraw's logo) with a claude-style asterix replacing the dot on top.
